In those days, a large part of what's now Turkey was considered to be just
as Greek as the now-Greek mainland and the isles. All over the
mediterranean there were Greek colonies (Naples is one of the best known
examples), including the eastern shore of the Black Sea (which the old
Greek sarcastically called "Pontos Euxeinos", the "hospitable sea",
because of its frequent storms).

So Dylan was correct: Medea *was* Greek, even if she didn't live in what
we would now call Greece.

And the Ukraine is the north-western shore of that sea, Russia the
north-eastern shore.

Colchis would be located on the north-eastern shore of presentday Turkey.

